{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000042","statement":"Achondrogenesis is a group of severe disorders, present from birth, that affect the development of cartilage and bone.","source_span":"Achondrogenesis is a group of severe disorders that are present from birth and affect the development of cartilage and bone."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000042","statement":"There are at least three forms of achondrogenesis: type 1A, type 1B, and type 2.","source_span":"There are at least three forms of achondrogenesis, type 1A, type 1B and type 2"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000042","statement":"Type 1A and type 1B achondrogenesis are both inherited in an autosomal recessive pattern.","source_span":"Type 1A and 1B achondrogenesis are both inherited in an autosomal recessive pattern."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000042","statement":"Type 1B achondrogenesis may be caused by mutations in the SLC26A2 gene.","source_span":"Type 1B may be caused by mutations in the SLC26A2 gene."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000042","statement":"Type 2 achondrogenesis is inherited in an autosomal dominant pattern and is caused by new (de novo) mutations in the COL2A1 gene.","source_span":"Type 2 achondrogenesis is inherited in an autosomal dominant pattern and is caused by new (de novo) mutations in the COL2A1 gene."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000042","statement":"The genetic cause of achondrogenesis type 1A remains unknown.","source_span":"The genetic cause of achondrogenesis type 1A remains unknown."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000042","statement":"The SLC26A2 gene provides instructions for a protein important for normal cartilage development and for converting cartilage to bone.","source_span":"The SLC26A2 gene provides instructions for making a protein that is important for the normal development of cartilage and for the conversion of cartilage to bone."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000042","statement":"The COL2A1 gene provides instructions for a protein that forms a type of collagen found mostly in cartilage and in the vitreous of the eyeball.","source_span":"The COL2A1 gene provides instructions for making a protein that forms a type of collagen found mostly in cartilage and in the clear gel that fills the eyeball (vitreous)."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000042","statement":"Achondrogenesis type 2 is almost always caused by new (de novo) mutations and typically occurs in people with no family history of the disorder.","source_span":"It is almost always caused by new (de novo) mutations and typically occurs in people with no history of the disorder in their family."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000042","fact_refs":[1],"question":"How many forms of achondrogenesis are there, and what are they called?","answer":"There are at least three forms of achondrogenesis: type 1A, type 1B, and type 2."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000042","fact_refs":[3,6],"question":"What gene is linked to achondrogenesis type 1B, and what does that gene normally do?","answer":"Type 1B achondrogenesis may be caused by mutations in the SLC26A2 gene, which normally provides instructions for a protein important for cartilage development and for converting cartilage into bone."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000042","fact_refs":[4,8],"question":"What causes achondrogenesis type 2 and how is it typically inherited?","answer":"Achondrogenesis type 2 is caused by new (de novo) mutations in the COL2A1 gene, inherited in an autosomal dominant pattern, and it typically occurs in people with no family history of the disorder."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000042","fact_refs":[5],"question":"Is the genetic cause of achondrogenesis type 1A known?","answer":"No, the genetic cause of achondrogenesis type 1A remains unknown."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000042","fact_refs":[2],"question":"What is the inheritance pattern for achondrogenesis types 1A and 1B?","answer":"Both type 1A and type 1B achondrogenesis are inherited in an autosomal recessive pattern."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000042","fact_refs":[7],"question":"What does the COL2A1 gene normally do in the body?","answer":"The COL2A1 gene provides instructions for making a protein that forms a type of collagen found mostly in cartilage and in the vitreous gel of the eyeball."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000046","statement":"Achondroplasia is caused by mutations in the FGFR3 gene.","source_span":"Achondroplasia is caused by mutations in the FGFR3 gene."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000046","statement":"Achondroplasia is inherited in an autosomal dominant fashion.","source_span":"It is inherited in an autosomal dominant fashion."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000046","statement":"About 80% of individuals with achondroplasia have parents of normal stature and are born with the condition as a result of a new (de novo) gene mutation.","source_span":"About 80% of individuals who have achondroplasia have parents with normal stature and are born with the condition as a result of a new (de novo) gene alteration (mutation)."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000046","statement":"Each individual with achondroplasia has a 50% chance, with each pregnancy, of passing on the mutated gene.","source_span":"Each individual with achondroplasia has a 50% chance, with each pregnancy, to pass on the mutated gene."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000046","statement":"Two specific mutations in the FGFR3 gene are responsible for almost all cases of achondroplasia.","source_span":"Two specific mutations in the FGFR3 gene are responsible for almost all cases of achondroplasia."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000046","statement":"In babies, apnea occurs when breathing stops for more than 15 seconds.","source_span":"In babies, apnea occurs when breathing stops for more than 15 seconds."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000046","statement":"A speech evaluation is recommended by age two years for children with achondroplasia.","source_span":"Speech evaluation by age two years"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000046","statement":"Recommendations for managing children with achondroplasia were outlined by the American Academy of Pediatrics Committee on Genetics.","source_span":"Recommendations for management of children with achondroplasia were outlined by the American Academy of Pediatrics Committee on Genetics."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000046","fact_refs":[0,4],"question":"What gene is responsible for achondroplasia, and how many specific mutations account for almost all cases?","answer":"Achondroplasia is caused by mutations in the FGFR3 gene, and two specific mutations in this gene are responsible for almost all cases."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000046","fact_refs":[2],"question":"What percentage of people with achondroplasia have parents of normal stature, and why does this happen?","answer":"About 80% of individuals with achondroplasia have parents of normal stature; they are born with the condition due to a new (de novo) gene mutation rather than inheriting it."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000046","fact_refs":[3],"question":"What is the chance that a person with achondroplasia will pass the mutated gene to their child in any given pregnancy?","answer":"Each individual with achondroplasia has a 50% chance, with each pregnancy, of passing on the mutated gene."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000046","fact_refs":[1],"question":"How is achondroplasia inherited when it is passed down?","answer":"When achondroplasia is inherited, it follows an autosomal dominant pattern of inheritance."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000046","fact_refs":[6],"question":"By what age should a child with achondroplasia receive a speech evaluation?","answer":"A speech evaluation is recommended by age two years."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000046","fact_refs":[5],"question":"How long must breathing stop in a baby for it to be classified as apnea?","answer":"In babies, apnea occurs when breathing stops for more than 15 seconds."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000114","statement":"Acute febrile neutrophilic dermatosis is also known as Sweet syndrome.","source_span":"Acute febrile neutrophilic dermatosis - also known as Sweet syndrome"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000114","statement":"It is a skin condition marked by fever, arthritis, and painful skin lesions that appear mainly on the face, neck, back and arms.","source_span":"a skin condition marked by fever, inflammation of the joints (arthritis), and painful skin lesions that appear mainly on the face, neck, back and arms"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000114","statement":"Middle-aged women are most likely to develop the condition, though it may also affect men, older adults, and infants.","source_span":"Although middle-aged women are most likely to develop this condition, it may also affect men, older adults and even infants."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000114","statement":"Most often the condition is not serious and will clear on its own within a few months.","source_span":"Most often, it isn't serious and will clear on its own in a few months."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000114","statement":"Doctors usually prescribe systemic corticosteroids, such as prednisone or prednisolone, to treat this condition.","source_span":"Doctors usually prescribe systemic corticosteroids (prednisone or prednisolone) to treat this condition."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000114","statement":"With medication, skin lesions can improve within two or three days, with the worst lesions disappearing within one to four weeks.","source_span":"Medications can improve skin lesions and associated symptoms in just two or three days, with the worst of the lesions disappearing within one to four weeks."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000114","statement":"Left untreated, the condition (when not associated with a more serious disease) may disappear on its own within one to three months.","source_span":"Left untreated, acute febrile neutrophilic dermatosis not associated with a more serious condition may disappear on its own within one to three months."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000114","statement":"The condition can be a sign of an immune response linked to acute myelogenous leukemia, inflammatory bowel disease, bowel or breast cancer, pregnancy, or rheumatoid arthritis, among others.","source_span":"Blood disorders, especially acute myelogenous leukemia, a cancer of the blood and bone marrow Inflammatory bowel disease, such as ulcerative colitis or Crohn's disease Bowel or breast cancer Pregnancy Rheumatoid arthritis"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000114","statement":"Certain medications, including nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs), can trigger the condition.","source_span":"Certain medications, including nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs)"}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000114","fact_refs":[0],"question":"What is another name for acute febrile neutrophilic dermatosis?","answer":"It is also known as Sweet syndrome."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000114","fact_refs":[2],"question":"Which group of people is most likely to develop acute febrile neutrophilic dermatosis?","answer":"Middle-aged women are most likely to develop the condition, though men, older adults, and even infants can also be affected."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000114","fact_refs":[4],"question":"What medications do doctors usually prescribe to treat acute febrile neutrophilic dermatosis?","answer":"Doctors usually prescribe systemic corticosteroids, such as prednisone or prednisolone."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000114","fact_refs":[5,6],"question":"How quickly do skin lesions from this condition resolve with treatment compared to without treatment?","answer":"With medication, lesions can improve within two to three days and the worst ones disappear within one to four weeks; left untreated, the condition may resolve on its own within one to three months."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000114","fact_refs":[7],"question":"What underlying health conditions can acute febrile neutrophilic dermatosis be a sign of?","answer":"It can be a sign of an immune response linked to conditions such as acute myelogenous leukemia, inflammatory bowel disease, bowel or breast cancer, pregnancy, or rheumatoid arthritis."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000114","fact_refs":[1],"question":"Which parts of the body are mainly affected by the painful skin lesions of acute febrile neutrophilic dermatosis?","answer":"The painful skin lesions appear mainly on the face, neck, back, and arms."}
